15:34:25.978104535 Stop {"session_id":"00000002-0000-4000-8000-000000000002","transcript_path":"/home/agent/.claude/projects/-tmp-claude-1000--home-dev-work-repositories-hq-00000004-0000-4000-8000-000000000004-scratchpad-r82-ws/00000002-0000-4000-8000-000000000002.jsonl","cwd":"/tmp/claude-1000/-home-dev-work-repositories-hq/00000004-0000-4000-8000-000000000004/scratchpad/r82/ws","scratchpad_dir":"/tmp/claude-1000/-tmp-claude-1000--home-dev-work-repositories-hq-00000004-0000-4000-8000-000000000004-scratchpad-r82-ws/00000002-0000-4000-8000-000000000002/scratchpad","prompt_id":"00000033-0000-4000-8000-000000000033","permission_mode":"bypassPermissions","effort":{"level":"high"},"hook_event_name":"Stop","stop_hook_active":false,"last_assistant_message":"**The Song That Came Back**\n\nFor eleven years, the whale sang a song no other whale could answer.\n\nScientists named her Fifty-Two, after the frequency of her call. At fifty-two hertz, it was far higher than the calls of the blue and fin whales she most resembled. Hydrophones across the Pacific picked up her strange, lonely note every autumn as she swam south and every spring as she swam north. She called and called, and nothing ever called back.\n\nMarisol Okafor had listened to Fifty-Two since graduate school. She knew the song the way some people know a voice on the radio: its rhythm, its pauses, the way it rose slightly at the end like a question. At night, alone in the lab with headphones on, she sometimes caught herself whispering, *I hear you*, as if that counted for something.\n\nThe funding ran out in October, and the hydrophone network would be shut down by the new year. Marisol spent her last weeks at the station cataloguing recordings, labeling hard drives, and saying goodbye to a sound she would never hear again.\n\nOn her final night, she put on the headphones one more time.\n\nFifty-Two was there, faint and far away, somewhere off the coast of northern California. Marisol closed her eyes and let the song wash over her: the long rising note, the pause, the question.\n\nThen, underneath it, she heard something else.\n\nShe sat up and played it back. She ran it through the spectrogram twice, then a third time, her hands shaking on the keyboard.\n\nIt was a second voice, also at fifty-two hertz. It wasn't an echo, because the timing was wrong and the pattern was different. It sounded younger, rougher at the edges, but it had the same strange frequency. And it wasn't just singing. It was answering. Fifty-Two called, the other voice called back, and Fifty-Two called again, faster now, as if she couldn't believe it either.\n\nMarisol listened until dawn. Through the night the two songs drew closer, threading through each other, until by morning she could barely tell them apart.\n\nShe never found out where the second whale came from. Maybe it was a calf, or another outlier born with the same odd voice. Some colleagues doubted the recording, and others said it changed nothing.\n\nBut Marisol kept a copy on her phone. On bad days she played it: two voices in the dark ocean, no longer alone.","background_tasks":[],"session_crons":[]}
